<section class="wrap" style="max-width:760px;padding:0 20px 40px">

  <p style="font-size:17px;line-height:1.75;color:#39414B">Writ Large is researched and written by <strong>Charles Christopher, a paralegal</strong> employed by Prisoner Legal Aid LLC, a Florida paralegal services company. The work here is federal post-conviction document preparation and legal research, not legal representation.</p>

  <div style="border-left:3px solid #F0B429;background:#f6f7f9;padding:16px 18px;margin:26px 0;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0">
    <p style="margin:0;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7"><strong>Important:</strong> A paralegal is not an attorney and cannot give legal advice, represent anyone in court, or predict how a court will rule. Nothing on this site creates an attorney-client relationship. Paralegal document preparation is performed under the oversight of a licensed attorney; the client remains self-represented.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 style="font-size:22px;margin:34px 0 10px">What this site covers</h2>
  <p style="font-size:16px;line-height:1.75;color:#39414B">Writ Large focuses narrowly on federal post-conviction relief and sentence execution:</p>
  <ul style="font-size:16px;line-height:1.85;color:#39414B;padding-left:22px">
    <li>Motions to vacate under 28 U.S.C. &sect; 2255</li>
    <li>Habeas petitions under &sect; 2241 (sentence execution) and &sect; 2254 (state judgments)</li>
    <li>Compassionate release under 18 U.S.C. &sect; 3582(c)(1)(A)</li>
    <li>First Step Act time credits and BOP sentence computation</li>
    <li>Filing deadlines, procedural default, exhaustion, and the prisoner mailbox rule</li>
    <li>Official federal court forms and filing procedure</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 style="font-size:22px;margin:34px 0 10px">How the legal content here is checked</h2>
  <p style="font-size:16px;line-height:1.75;color:#39414B">Substantive legal statements are checked against primary authority rather than secondary summaries. In practice that means:</p>
  <ul style="font-size:16px;line-height:1.85;color:#39414B;padding-left:22px">
    <li><strong>Statutes</strong> are read from the U.S. Code as published by the Office of the Law Revision Counsel.</li>
    <li><strong>Supreme Court decisions</strong> are checked against the opinion text, not a summary of it, including the specific holding relied on.</li>
    <li><strong>Federal forms</strong> come from uscourts.gov and are linked to their official source. Government forms are never recreated or reformatted here.</li>
    <li><strong>BOP policy</strong> is taken from the Bureau of Prisons or the Federal Register.</li>
  </ul>
  <p style="font-size:16px;line-height:1.75;color:#39414B">The full method is set out in the <a href="/editorial-standards/">editorial standards</a>.</p>

  <h2 style="font-size:22px;margin:34px 0 10px">Attorney oversight</h2>
  <p style="font-size:16px;line-height:1.75;color:#39414B">Paralegal document preparation is performed under the oversight of a licensed attorney; the client remains self-represented. Articles on this site are written by a paralegal and are general information, not legal advice.</p>

  <h2 style="font-size:22px;margin:34px 0 10px">Experience</h2>
  <p style="font-size:16px;line-height:1.8;color:#39414B">Charles Christopher has worked in federal post-conviction and prisoner civil rights matters for several years. His work covers motions to vacate under &sect; 2255, &sect; 2241 habeas petitions, compassionate release motions, Federal Tort Claims Act administrative claims, civil rights complaints under &sect; 1983, Bureau of Prisons administrative appeals, and certificate of appealability applications, in federal district courts and courts of appeals across many jurisdictions.</p>
  <p style="font-size:16px;line-height:1.8;color:#39414B">That work is why the articles here spend so much time on deadlines, exhaustion and procedure. In practice, more federal post-conviction filings are lost to a missed date or a skipped step than to a weak argument, and the families who contact us usually learn about those rules after the fact.</p>

  <h2 style="font-size:22px;margin:34px 0 10px">Who these articles are written for</h2>
  <p style="font-size:16px;line-height:1.8;color:#39414B">Most people who read Writ Large are not the person in prison. They are wives, mothers, sisters and daughters trying to understand a system that rarely explains itself, often from a phone late at night. The articles are written for that reader: each one starts from a question a family actually asks, answers it in plain language first, and then gives the deadline that applies and what can be done this week.</p>

  <h2 style="font-size:22px;margin:34px 0 10px">How an article is written and kept current</h2>
  <ul style="font-size:16px;line-height:1.85;color:#39414B;padding-left:22px">
    <li>Every legal statement is checked against the statute, regulation or court opinion it rests on before the article is published.</li>
    <li>When the law changes, as it did with the Supreme Court's 2026 compassionate release decisions, affected articles are revised.</li>
    <li>Corrections are made as soon as an error is found. The process is described in the <a href="/editorial-standards/">editorial standards</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 style="font-size:22px;margin:34px 0 10px">What you will not find here</h2>
  <ul style="font-size:16px;line-height:1.85;color:#39414B;padding-left:22px">
    <li>Predictions about how a specific case will come out.</li>
    <li>Any suggestion that hiring anyone changes the odds.</li>
    <li>Guarantees of any kind. Most post-conviction motions are denied, and this site says so where it is relevant.</li>
  </ul>

